item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"In stable SCOPe 2.08, NAD(P)-associated domains occupy genuinely different structural groups: the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann superfamily c.2.1 (SUNID 51735) lies in fold c.2; the DHS-like NAD/FAD-binding superfamily c.31.1 (SUNID 52467) lies in fold c.31; and the HMG-CoA-reductase NAD-binding family d.58.20.1 (SUNID 55036) lies in superfamily d.58.20 and the ferredoxin-like fold d.58, even in a different SCOPe class. SCOPe also notes that c.31.1 binds its cofactor in the opposite direction from the classical Rossmann fold, while c.2 has nucleotide-binding modes similar to nearby folds.

The evolutionary implication is level-dependent. Members of one family have the closest SCOPe relationship; membership in one superfamily supports probable common ancestry followed by divergence, although it does not establish identical chemistry. Across different superfamilies—and especially different folds—the recurrence of NAD(P) binding is consistent with convergent recruitment of the same chemically useful cofactor by distinct structural solutions. It is not, however, proof that every such pair evolved independently: the retrieved fold records explicitly say that homology is not implied above the superfamily level, which is an absence of a positive SCOPe homology assertion rather than proof of nonhomology.

Cofactor binding or local active-site resemblance alone is insufficient because selection by the same ligand and reaction chemistry can favor similar local geometries or short motifs without shared ancestry; local resemblance does not establish correspondence across the whole domain core. Conversely, divergent homologs can retain ancestry while changing substrate, NAD versus NADP preference, catalytic residues, mechanism, or overall function. Therefore one must not transfer exact reaction, substrate specificity, cofactor preference, stereochemistry, catalytic mechanism, whole-protein function, or assume interchangeable structural templates from NAD(P) binding or active-site similarity alone. Stronger convergence or remote-homology claims require external global structural alignments and topology, statistically supported sequence correspondence across equivalent core positions, motifs compared in equivalent structural contexts, verified domain boundaries and architectures, phylogenetic evidence, and ligand-bound biochemical or mutational evidence. Template selection should remain within a verified homologous family or superfamily where possible and still be checked for domain context, active-site conservation, ligand state, and biochemical specificity.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD&mode=token&limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52466/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/55036","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/55036/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/55036/homology","scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md"]}]}
